,
 the study hypothesizes that male-headed households achieve higher maize productivity and gross sales revenue than female-headed households,
 that these gaps persist after observable characteristics are taken into account,
 and that the structural component exceeds the component explained by differences in endowments.
 The paper provides policy relevant evidence on the factors underlying the differences in agricultural outcomes between MHH and FHH cultivating maize.
 Understanding these gender differentiated constraints is essential for achieving an inclusive agricultural transformation,
 improving welfare and food security.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results show that female-headed households achieve significantly lower maize productivity and revenue than male-headed households,
 and that these gaps persist even after accounting for observable differences in household and plot characteristics.
 The Oaxaca–Blinder decomposition indicates that unequal returns to resources,
 rather than unequal access to them,
 account for the majority of both gaps.
 The qualitative evidence points to time constraints ,
 technology adoption and gendered market discrimination as key mechanisms underlying these structural disparities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This study contributes to the literature on gender gaps in smallholder maize production by drawing on a high-quality household panel dataset with detailed household- and plot-level information from the Busoga region,
 directly comparing male- and female-headed households.
 In addition to examining gender gaps in maize productivity and gross sales revenue,
 the analysis distinguishes between market participation and gross sales revenue conditional on selling,
 allowing gender disparities in commercialization to be examined along the extensive and intensive margins.
 Finally,
 the study combines quantitative decomposition methods with qualitative evidence from focus group discussions to help contextualize the mechanisms underlying these gaps.
\end_layout

