\begin_layout Subsection
Quantitative Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Quantitative Sample
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper uses panel data from three survey rounds with approximately 3,500 smallholder maize farmers in Busoga region.
 The data were originally collected within a broader research project examining agro-input dealers and farmer interactions in Uganda (De et al.,
 2024;
 Miehe et al.,
 2025,
 2026).
 The agro-dealer sample was constructed by listing all agro-dealers operating across eleven districts in the Busoga region,
 an area characterized by smallholder maize production,
 where maize serves both as a staple food and a cash crop.
 To link agro-dealers with farmers,
 each agro-dealer was asked to identify the villages where most of their customers reside.
 Enumerators then randomly sampled ten maize-growing households from each of these villages,
 yielding the sample of 3,470 farmers.
 Enumerators interviewed the household member most knowledgeable about maize cultivation.
 A subset of questions specifically targeted the household head,
 who may or may not have been the respondent.
 In addition to general information on agricultural practices,
 farmers were asked to list all their maize plots,
 from which one was randomly selected for detailed data collection.
 This approach minimized survey duration and respondent burden while preserving representativeness at the household level,
 due to the random selection of plots.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first round of data were collected in April 2021,
 followed by the second round of surveys in January-February 2022 and third round of surveys in July-August 2022.
 Of the 3,470 farmers interviewed in the first round of data collection,
 3,407 were re-interviewed at second one and 3,441 at the third round.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dataset also identifies the catchment area in which each household is located.
 Catchment areas represent the relevant local spatial units used in the decomposition analysis to account for shared characteristics among households located in the same area.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Measurement of Outcome Variables and Covariates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The analysis focuses on two main outcome variables:
 productivity and gross sales revenues from maize.
 Both outcome variables are measured on the randomly selected plot.
 Agricultural productivity is measured as maize production per unit of cultivated land.
 Maize gross revenue is measured as the gross value of maize sales,
 expressed in Ugandan shillings.
 For households that did not sell maize,
 revenue is set to zero.
 For households that reported selling maize,
 income is calculated as the quantity sold multiplied by the unit price received.
 The resulting measure therefore captures both the extensive margin of market participation and the intensive margin of sales among participating households.
 Observations with missing information on sold quantities or bag prices among sellers are left as missing.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Comment
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Hybrid seeds adoption is measured as a binary indicator equal to one if the household reports using one of the officially recognized hybrid maize varieties (Longe 10H,
 Longe 7H,
 Longe 7R Kayongo-go,
 Bazooka,
 Longe 6H,
 Panner,
 Wema,
 or KH series) on the randomly selected plot during the reference season,
 and zero otherwise.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the skewed distribution and the presence of zero values in the outcome variables,
 maize productivity and gross sales revenues are transformed using the inverse hyperbolic sine (IHS) transformation in the empirical analysis.
 Skewness does not violate a specific assumption of the Oaxaca–Blinder decomposition.
 However,
 because the standard decomposition relies on linear models of conditional means and relies on coefficients estimated from linear models (Fortin et al.,
 2011),
 extreme values may strongly influence the estimated mean gaps and regression coefficients.
 The IHS transformation reduces their influence while retaining zero observations.
 Skewness is according to the adjusted Fisher-Pearson coefficient of skewness exceeding 1.96.
 To address the recent critiques by Mullahy and Norton (2022) we applied the IHS transformation cautiously when dealing with revenue that have many zeros.
 For larger values of the outcomes,
 the IHS behaves similarly to a logarithmic transformation,
 although percentage interpretations should be treated as approximates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The distribution of maize productivity was inspected to identify potentially influential observations arising from unusually high reported harvests relative to plot area.
 Small plot areas can mechanically increase measured productivity and amplify measurement error.
 However,
 most extreme productivity observations are driven primarily by unusually high reported harvest quantities,
 rather than by a generalized presence of very small plot areas.
 Since these observations are internally consistent with the original survey responses and have only a small influence on the gender gap in IHS-transformed productivity,
 they are retained in the main analysis and excluded only in a robustness check in the 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Appendix"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Gross maize sales revenue is highly right-skewed.
 We examined whether extreme reported prices influenced the results.
 While excluding these observations reduced the gender gap in raw gross revenue,
 the gap changed only slightly once revenue was transformed using the IHS.
 This suggests that the IHS transformation effectively limits the influence of extreme values on the econometric results.
 Separating the intensive margin from the extensive margin further helps address this skewness,
 since restricting the analysis to households with positive sales removes the concentration of zero values that contributes most heavily to the right-skewed distribution of the pooled revenue outcome.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main covariates analysed in the dataset cover access to agricultural inputs markets,
 household demographic and socioeconomic characteristics,
 farm and land endowments,
 plot-level input use and management practices,
 maize sales.
 These covariates were selected based on the literature (section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec3 -Literature Review"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 as well as their availability in the dataset.
 They capture key observable factors that may influence maize productivity and gross sales revenue.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The descriptive statistics reported in table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "descriptive statistics"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 in subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Descriptive-Statistics"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 summarize the outcome variables and covariates used in the analysis.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Quantitative Data Limitations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This study adopts the inter-household approach because gender information is available only for the household head.
 Household headship does not necessarily reflect who manages the plot,
 controls productive assets,
 or makes agricultural decisions,
 and may therefore overlook important intra-household dynamics (Udry,
 1996;
 Ali et al.,
 2016).
 The qualitative evidence complements this analysis by providing insights into decision-making and power relations within households.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The paper can rely on relatively few female-headed households,
 which limits statistical power of the analysis.
 In the first round of data collection,
 we recorded the gender of the household head,
 identifying 774 female-headed households (22% of 3,470 farmers).
 At the last round of data collection,
 we recorded 760 female household heads (23% of 3,299).
 No information on the gender of the household head was collected at the midline stage;
 however,
 it is unlikely that there were any substantial changes during this period.
 Therefore,
 for the midline analysis,
 the gender classification observed at baseline and endline was assumed to remain unchanged.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The selected covariates capture key observable factors that may influence maize productivity and gross sales revenue.
 However,
 the analysis cannot account for all relevant factors,
 including soil quality and weather conditions,
 such as rainfall variability or irrigation intensity.
 These omitted factors may contribute to the unexplained component of the Oaxaca–Blinder decomposition,
 which should therefore not be interpreted as discrimination alone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Qualitative Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Qualitative Sample
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The qualitative component of the data is based on fieldwork conducted in the Busoga region in south-east Uganda,
 the same region covered by the quantitative data collection.
 Taking place over 15 days between November and December 2025,
 the fieldwork covered 16 villages in the districts of Iganga,
 Bugweri,
 Bugiri and Mayuge.
 16 focus group discussions (FGDs) were held,
 each with 10 participants (160 participants in total).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The composition of the groups was defined to capture specific gender perspectives,
 therefore 5 FGDs were women-only,
 5 were men-only,
 and 6 were mixed.
 The single-gender groups were used to encourage the participants to freely express experiences and opinions by reducing possible effects of bias and power asymmetries that can arise in mixed contexts.
 On the other hand,
 mixed groups allowed us to observe how intra-family norms and roles are reproduced in the discussion.
 All participants were adults and a different range of ages was ensured.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Qualitative Content and Approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The discussions were guided by a semi-structured outline consisting mainly of open-ended questions,
 probes and follow-ups.
 Moreover,
 to stimulate an open and participatory discussion,
 participants were encouraged to reflect on concrete examples and real-life situations.
 The questions covered topics related to gender norms,
 the division of labour and time use,
 economic empowerment,
 decision-making processes,
 power dynamics within the households,
 the adoption of new technology,
 and market access.
 All FGDs were conducted in Lusoga language by two enumerators,
 one male and one female,
 and lasted an average of about two hours.
 In all of them it was ensured privacy and confidentiality.
 In particular,
 women and male partners were not involved in the same session as this could compromise safety or freedom of expression.
 The qualitative work aimed to contextualize the quantitative results and explore underlying social mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Qualitative Data Limitations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FGDs were conducted in Lusoga and later translated.
 Therefore,
 nuances,
 humor,
 and culturally embedded meanings can be lost or reshaped through translation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The enumerator's male gender may have limited openness in women-only groups,
 particularly on themes like home conflict,
 threats,
 and sexual duties.
 Moreover,
 the occasional presence of local leaders (e.g.
 chairperson) can further influence answers toward community-approved narratives and diminish participants' willingness to criticize norms.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although the FGD participants came from the same villages as the survey respondents,
 they were not the same people.
 Therefore,
 the qualitative findings should be interpreted as complementary contextual evidence rather than as directly matched to the survey data.
 Moreover,
 participants were not necessarily household heads.
 The qualitative findings therefore do not directly represent the experiences of FHH and MHH,
 but they provide important insights into what it means to be a woman or a man in the villages included in the study and into the prevailing gendered norms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The qualitative findings are highly context-specific,
 including gender norms,
 labor arrangements,
 and market interactions unique to the Busoga region in Eastern Uganda.
 Nevertheless,
 the revealed mechanisms may help us to understand gender-based restrictions in similar rural settings,
 assuming that contextual differences are carefully recognised.
 
\end_layout

