\begin_layout Subsection
Oaxaca-Blinder Decomposition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To investigate the sources of gender disparities in productivity,
 gross sales revenue and in the intensive margin of gross sales revenue we apply the Oaxaca-Blinder decomposition (Oaxaca,
 1973;
 Blinder,
 1973).
 The OLS regressions provide the basis for the decomposition methods.
 This technique decomposes observed gender gaps into two components:
 the endowment effect and the structural effect.
 The endowment effect captures differences due to observable characteristics,
 such as access to land,
 credit,
 education,
 and inputs.
 The structural effect accounts for differences in returns to those resources,
 potentially reflecting discriminatory social norms or practices.
 The Oaxaca–Blinder decomposition therefore quantifies the contribution of observable characteristics available in the household survey data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on the work of Kilic et al.
 (2015),
 we derived the average gap between MHH and FHH "D",
 expressed as the average difference in productivity and gross revenue:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D=E(Y_{m})-E(Y_{f})\label{eq:one-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{m}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{f}$
\end_inset

 denote the expected outcomes for MHH and FHH,
 obtained from linear estimation of agricultural productivity and revenue expressed in inverse hyperbolic sine (IHS) units.
