 such as land quality or agro-ecological conditions.
 Therefore,
 the structural component should be interpreted with caution as reflecting differential returns rather than direct evidence of discrimination (Fortin et al.,
 2011).
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:one-1-1-3"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 represent a classic Oaxaca-Blinder twofold decomposition,
 while the threefold Oaxaca-Blinder decomposition can be obtained by further separating the coefficient component into male and female sub-components.
 The male and female coefficient components measure how the coefficient structures deviate from a pooled benchmark 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 To construct the benchmark as a counterfactual non-discriminatory structure 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{*}$
\end_inset

,
 we use the coefficients from a pooled regression estimated on the combined sample of MHH and FHH (Jann,
 2008).
 This approach avoids privileging either group’s returns structure as the benchmark and instead relies on the average returns observed in the overall production environment,
 under the assumption that male and female farmers operate within a similar agricultural system and production function.
 As a sensitivity analysis,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Appendix"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reports the decomposition using the MHH and FHH coefficient structures as alternative benchmarks.
 Moreover,
 given the imbalance in the number of observations across the two groups,
 the Appendix also reports a pooled decomposition in which MHH and FHH receive equal group weights when estimating the benchmark coefficient structure.
 This allows us to assess whether the results are sensitive to the greater representation of MHH in the pooled sample.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Expanding the decomposition across covariates k= 0,...,
 K,
 including the intercept,
 the total gap can be written as the contributions of each observable characteristics.
 The result is a detailed decomposition written as:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D=\sum_{k=0}^{K}(\bar{X}_{m,k}-\bar{X}_{f,k})\beta_{k}^{*}+\sum_{k=0}^{K}\bar{X}_{m,k}(\beta_{m,k}-\beta_{k}^{*})+\sum_{k=0}^{K}\bar{X}_{f,k}(\beta_{k}^{*}-\beta_{f,k})\label{eq:one-1-1-3-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\lang american
where each covariate 𝑘 contributes separately to the endowment effect and to the two structural components.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Detailed decomposition components should not be interpreted as causal effects of individual covariates.
 Rather,
 they show how differences in observable characteristics and differences in estimated coefficient structures contribute mechanically to the overall gap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To account for unobserved spatial heterogeneity,
 the decompositions include catchment-area fixed effects.
 This is implemented by applying a within transformation to the outcome and covariates before estimating the decomposition.
 For each variable 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

,
 the transformed variable is defined as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{Z_{hc}}​=Z_{hc}​-\bar{Z}_{c}+\bar{Z}\label{eq:one-1-1-3-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{Z_{hc}}$
\end_inset

 is the value of the variable 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 for household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 in catchment area 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{Z}_{c}$
\end_inset

 is the catchment-area mean,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{Z}$
\end_inset

 is the overall sample mean.
 The fixed effect remove average differences across catchment areas.
 The decomposition is then applied to the transformed variables.
 This specification compares MHH and FHH within similar local production environments and helps accounting for unobserved catchment-level characteristics such as agro-ecological conditions or other area-specific factors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Standard errors are estimated using bootstrap replications clustered at the catchment-area level.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Fairlie Decomposition of Maize Market Participation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Oaxaca–Blinder decomposition described above is based on linear outcome models and cannot be directly applied to the binary extensive-margin outcome.
 To decompose the gender gap in maize market participation,
 we therefore use the nonlinear decomposition proposed by Fairlie (1999,
 2005),
 which extends the logic of the Oaxaca–Blinder approach to binary outcomes estimated using logit or probit models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang english
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D_{s}=\bar{S_{m}}-\bar{S_{f}}\label{eq:one-1-1-4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{h}$
\end_inset

 is equal to one if household h sold maize and zero otherwise.
 The participation gap is therefore defined as the difference in the average probability of selling maize between MHH and FHH.
 This gap can be decomposed into an explained and an unexplained component,
 following Fairlie's (1999,
 2005) extension of the Oaxaca–Blinder approach to binary outcomes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D_{S}^{E}=\frac{1}{N_{m}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{m}}\varLambda(X_{mi}\hat{\beta^{*})}-\frac{1}{N_{f}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{f}}\varLambda(X_{fi}\hat{\beta^{*})}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\varLambda()$
\end_inset

 denotes the logistic cumulative distribution function,
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta^{*}}$
\end_inset

 comes from a logit model estimated on the pooled sample of MHH and FHH (Fairlie,
 2005).
 To compute 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{s}^{E}$
\end_inset

,
 we calculate each group's average predicted probability of market participation using that group's own observed characteristics,
 but the same pooled coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 for both groups.
 Because both terms share the same coefficients,
 applying the identical decision rule to both groups,
 any difference between the two averages can only come from differences in observed characteristics —
 not from differences in how those characteristics translate into outcomes.
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{s}^{E}$
\end_inset

 therefore captures the explained component of the participation gap:
 the part associated with MHH and FHH differing in observable characteristics such as land,
 education,
 or market access.
 The remaining component,
 
\begin_inset Formula $Ds-D_{s}^{E}$
\end_inset

,
 captures the part of the gap that persists even when MHH and FHH share the same distribution of covariates,
 evaluated under the pooled coefficient structure.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As previously done with the detailed decomposition,
 the explained component is further decomposed into contributions associated with individual covariates.
 This is done by sequentially replacing each covariate's female values with the corresponding male values,
 one variable at a time,
 and recording the resulting change in the average predicted probability at each step.
 Because the logistic function is nonlinear,
 the estimated contribution of a given covariate depends on the values already substituted for the other covariates at that point,
 therefore switching education before land,
 for instance,
 can have a different contribution than switching land before education,
 even though the two must sum to the same total.
 To avoid results driven by an arbitrary sequence,
 the ordering of covariates is randomized repeatedly and the reported contributions are averaged across repetitions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the MHH and FHH samples differ in size,
 the larger group is randomly subsampled to match the size of the smaller group,
 and observations are then matched after ranking households by their predicted probability of market participation;
 this subsampling is repeated across randomization draws to avoid dependence on a single random subsample.
 The validity of this rank-based matching procedure depends on sufficient overlap in predicted probabilities between MHH and FHH;
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 shows substantial overlap across most of the probability range,
 supporting the appropriateness of the matching approach.
\end_layout

