 report OLS regression results on the determinants of maize productivity and maize gross sales revenue,
 disaggregated by gender of the household head.
 Gross sales revenue is analyzed along two margins,
 as described in the empirical strategy.
 Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:OLS-estimates-of-1-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reports results for the extensive margin,
 estimated using a logit model as described in the empirical strategy,
 while Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:OLS-estimates-of-1-1-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reports results for the intensive margin,
 the gross sales revenue among households that do sell.
 For each outcome,
 Column (1) reports the raw gender gap estimated from a bivariate regression including only an indicator for male-headed households.
 Column (2) presents results from the full baseline sample controlling for household characteristics,
 access to input markets,
 and plot-level production factors.
 Columns (3) and (4) report results from the full baseline specification estimated separately on the samples of male-headed and female-headed households,
 respectively,
 allowing returns to observable characteristics to differ by gender.
 Columns (5)–(7) present analogous pooled OLS regressions estimated on the combined sample of all survey rounds,
 including round fixed effects.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
OLS - Productivity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:OLS-estimates-of"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents regression results for maize productivity measured as production per acre on the randomly selected plot.
 Column (1) shows a sizable and statistically significant raw gender gap:
 maize productivity on male-headed households is approximately 20 %
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For sufficiently large values of the dependent variable,
 coefficients from regressions using IHS-transformed outcomes can be interpreted approximately as percentage changes (Bellemare and Wichman,
 2020).
 using the transformation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
100×(𝑒𝛽^−1)100×(eβ−1).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 higher than on female-headed plots.
 Importantly,
 this gap remains statistically significant and of similar magnitude after controlling for a comprehensive set of household and plot-level characteristics (Column 2),
 indicating that observable endowments explain only a limited share of the productivity differential and pointing instead to differences in returns and unobserved constraints disproportionately affecting female farmers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Several plot-level inputs are positively associated with maize productivity.
 Fertilizer use,
 particularly Urea,
 DAP/NPK application,
 and the use of organic manure,
 chemical inputs and hybrid seeds on the randomly selected plot are strongly correlated with higher yields,
 although the gender-differentiated regression reveal some heterogeneity in returns to these inputs.
 In particular urea usage has really high returns on productivity for FHH.
 Education of the household head is positively associated with maize productivity among female-headed households,
 while the corresponding effect is small and statistically insignificant among male-headed households,
 suggesting that education may play a more important role in increasing productivity among female farmers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When pooling observations across survey rounds,
 the estimated gender coefficient remains positive and statistically significant,
 confirming that the productivity gap between male and female headed households persist within a larger sample,
 even after accounting for round-fixed effects.
 In particular,
 household size and the age of the household head become consistently and negatively associated with maize productivity,
 when considering the larger sample.
 The use of quality seeds and fertilizer inputs,
 including both DAP/NPK and urea,
 remains strongly and positively associated with productivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reduction in the estimation sample is primarily driven by missing information on the number of agro-input shops and the distance to the nearest agro-input shop.
 Of the 362 observations excluded from the full specification,
 259 are lost exclusively because one or both of these variables are missing.
\end_layout

