\begin_layout Subsubsection
Extensive and Intensive Margin of Gross Sales Revenue
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:OLS-estimates-of-1-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 examines the extensive margin of maize commercialization at the household level.
 At baseline,
 MHH are 15.6 % points more likely than FHH to sell maize.
 This gap narrows to 9.9 % points once household characteristics,
 market access,
 plot size,
 and input use are controlled for,
 but remains statistically significant at the 1% level.
 This shows that observed characteristics explain part of the raw gap,
 but not all of it,
 taking these covariates into consideration.
 The pooled estimates show the same pattern,
 with MHH remaining 5.9 % points more likely to participate in the maize market after controls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Plot area and quality-seed use are both strongly and positively associated with market participation,
 while household size is negatively associated with it in both the baseline and pooled specifications.
 Age matters specifically for the decision to sell:
 older household heads are less likely to participate in the market.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:OLS-estimates-of-1-1-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 turns to the intensive margin,
 restricting the sample to households that sold maize and reported positive gross sales revenue.
 Even within this group,
 MHH earn more than FHH:
 the raw baseline gap is 0.328 IHS units,
 or approximately 38.8% in percentage terms²,
 falling to 0.209 IHS units (23.2%) once household- and plot-level characteristics are included,
 and remaining significant at the 1% level.
 The pooled estimate is smaller still,
 at 0.107 IHS units (11.3%),
 but remains statistically significant.
 As with the extensive margin,
 observed characteristics narrow the conditional gap without closing it,
 and a statistically significant gap persists in both cases —
 evidence that observable differences alone cannot account for the full gender gap in maize commercialization.
\end_layout

