\begin_layout Subsubsection
Extensive margin and Intensive Margin of Gross Sales Revenue
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Aggregate-Fairlie-decomposition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents the Fairlie decomposition of the gender gap in maize market participation.
 MHH sell maize at a rate of 55.1%,
 compared with 41.0% for FHH —
 a 14.0 percentage-point gap.
 Differences in observed characteristics account for 7.4 percentage points of this gap (52.6%),
 while the remaining 6.7 percentage points (47.4%) are not explained by observed characteristics.
 Both components are statistically significant at the 1% level,
 indicating that observable differences and unexplained factors play roughly equal roles in the participation gap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The detailed decomposition in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Detailed-Fairlie-decomposition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 shows which characteristics drive the explained portion.
 Plot area contributes the most,
 accounting for 3.3 percentage points of the gap.
 Differences in education and quality-seed use also contribute positively,
 adding around 1.0 and 0.6 percentage points respectively.
 Household size works in the opposite direction,
 narrowing the gap by about 1.0 percentage point.
 Households with more members are,
 on average,
 less likely to sell maize in the market,
 regardless of gender,
 and since MHH tend to have larger households than FHH,
 this works to narrow the participation gap rather than widen it.
 The remaining characteristics contribute only marginally to the explained gap.
 Unlike the linear Oaxaca–Blinder decomposition,
 the Fairlie method does not provide a well-defined procedure for decomposing the coefficient (unexplained) component into contributions from individual covariates,
 because switching coefficients one at a time is highly sensitive to ordering in a nonlinear model.
 The detailed decomposition is therefore reported only for the endowment component (Table 11),
 consistent with standard practice in the literature applying this method (Fairlie,
 2005)
\end_layout

