 reports the Oaxaca–Blinder decomposition of gross sales revenue among maize sellers.
 The gender gap here is 0.253 IHS units.
 Differences in observed characteristics explain 0.071 units of this gap (28.0%),
 while differences in estimated coefficients explain 0.182 units (72.0%).
 Therefore,
 the coefficient component accounts for the larger share of the conditional revenue gap.
 Within this component,
 the female coefficient effect alone accounts for 57.6% of the total gap,
 compared with 14.4% for the male coefficient effect,
 suggesting that the returns FHH earn from their characteristics,
 more than the characteristics themselves,
 drive most of the unexplained gap among sellers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The detailed decomposition in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Detailed-twofold-decomposition-2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 identifies plot area as the main driver on both sides of the decomposition:
 it contributes 0.056 IHS units to the endowment component and 0.343 IHS units to the structural component,
 making it the single largest contributor to the revenue gap among sellers overall.
 Distance to the nearest agro-input shop and organic manure use also show positive structural contributions,
 while re-sowing contributes negatively.
 Urea use has a small but significant negative endowment contribution,
 meaning that differences in urea use between MHH and FHH sellers slightly narrow,
 rather than widen,
 the gap.
 Most of the remaining variable-level contributions are not statistically significant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Comparing these results to the full-sample revenue decomposition discussed above (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Detailed-twofold-decomposition-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) reveals an important nuance:
 quality-seed use,
 which contributed significantly to both the endowment and structural components in the full sample,
 shows no significant contribution among sellers alone (0.017 and -0.083,
 both not significant).
 This suggests that quality seed matters primarily for revenue through its association with market participation itself,
 rather than through returns among households that already sell.
 Similarly,
 household size,
 a significant driver of the participation gap in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Detailed-Fairlie-decomposition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 is not a significant contributor to the revenue gap among sellers (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Detailed-twofold-decomposition-2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 reinforcing the idea that it operates mainly at the extensive margin.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Comparing the two margins reveals a different pattern at each stage of commercialization.
 For market participation,
 observed differences between MHH and FHH explain slightly more than half of the gap.
 Among households that already participate in the market,
 however,
 observed characteristics explain a much smaller share of the revenue gap,
 while differences in estimated returns to those characteristics account for most of it.
 This suggests that the gender gap in maize commercialization operates through two distinct mechanisms:
 unequal access to resources shapes who enters the market,
 while unequal returns to those same resources shape how much sellers earn once they are in it.
\end_layout

